<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ottokar II of Bohemia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1233–<a href="/years/1278">1278</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Přemysl Otakar II · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/premyslid-dynasty">Přemyslid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ottokar II was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> from 1253, known as the Iron and Golden King for his military strength and wealth. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1251 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a> after the Babenberg line died out and built a realm stretching to the Adriatic. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-i-of-germany">Rudolf of Habsburg</a> stripped him of these lands and defeated and killed him at the Marchfeld in 1278.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ottokar-ii-of-bohemia.jpg" alt="Ottokar II of Bohemia" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Otakarus · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PecetpoII.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ottokar was the second son of King Wenceslaus I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and of Kunigunde, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-of-swabia">Philip of Swabia</a>, through whom he was related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> emperors. He was first educated for an ecclesiastical career, while his elder brother Vladislaus was designated heir. When Vladislaus died in 1247, Ottokar became heir and was appointed Margrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, residing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a> and working on the reconstruction of lands devastated by the Mongol raids of 1242.</p><p>In 1248 discontented nobles who supported Emperor Frederick II drew him into a rebellion against his father. He was elected the younger king on 31 July 1248 and briefly expelled Wenceslaus from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague-castle">Prague Castle</a>. Pope Innocent IV excommunicated him, and Wenceslaus defeated the rebels and imprisoned his son at Přimda Castle before the two were reconciled.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The father and son turned to the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, left without a ruler after the last Babenberg duke, Frederick II, was killed in 1246. In 1251 the Austrian estates acclaimed Ottokar as duke, and on 11 February 1252 he married the duke's sister Margaret of Babenberg, who was much older than he. He succeeded to the Bohemian throne in 1253. Papal mediation settled a conflict with his cousin King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-iv-of-hungary">Béla IV of Hungary</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a>, and in July 1260 Ottokar defeated the Hungarians at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kressenbrunn">Battle of Kressenbrunn</a>. He then married Kunigunda of Halych, Béla's granddaughter, and she became the mother of his children, among them Wenceslaus II.</p><p>During the imperial interregnum he occupied the Egerland in 1266, inherited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a> from Ulrich III of Carinthia in 1269 and acquired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friuli">Friuli</a> in 1272. His bids for the imperial crown failed, and in 1273 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-i-of-germany">Rudolf of Habsburg</a>. Rudolf ordered the return of imperial lands, placed Ottokar under the imperial ban in 1275 and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. A rebellion led by Záviš of Falkenstein weakened Ottokar, and in November 1276 he gave up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and its neighbouring duchies, keeping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>. In 1278 he tried to regain them and was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-on-the-marchfeld">Battle on the Marchfeld</a> on 26 August.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ottokar's body was laid out in the Minorites Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-vitus-cathedral">St Vitus Cathedral</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> in 1297. His six-year-old son Wenceslaus II succeeded him. He founded about thirty towns, granted them charters and encouraged German-speaking settlers. He built or promoted castles such as Zvíkov, Křivoklát and Bezděz. The Zemské desky, the oldest preserved source of Czech law, dates from his time.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ottokar II of Bohemia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Teutonic Knights’ fortress of Königsberg, founded in 1255, is traditionally said to have been named in honour of King <b>Ottokar II of Bohemia</b>, who had joined their crusade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Afterwards Béla IV encouraged stone castles, which helped repel a second Mongol invasion in 1285–1286, and in 1278 Ladislaus IV's cavalry helped Rudolf of Habsburg defeat <b>Ottokar II of Bohemia</b> on the Marchfeld.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Mongol raiders crossed it in 1241, and in 1278 <b>Ottokar II of Bohemia</b>, who had used Moravia as a base for his Austrian ambitions, was killed fighting Rudolf of Habsburg at the Battle on the Marchfeld, just beyond the Moravian border.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Margraviate of Moravia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Königsberg was founded in 1255 during a crusade led by King <b>Ottokar II of Bohemia</b> and named in his honour.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At Dürnkrut and Jedenspeigen, the German king Rudolf I of Habsburg, allied with King Ladislaus IV of Hungary, defeated and killed <b>Ottokar II of Bohemia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-26">26 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Count Rudolf IV was elected King of the Romans in 1273 as Rudolf I and in 1278 defeated <b>Ottokar II of Bohemia</b>, gaining Austria and Styria.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-habsburg">County of Habsburg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Městec Králové, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. Died in Dürnkrut, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.21,15.3&amp;year=1262" aria-label="Městec Králové on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ottokar II of Bohemia"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4204 5745h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4204 5745h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.21,15.3&amp;year=1262">What was here in 1262? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
